Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Rapidly progressive dementia, Stimulus-sensitive myoclonus, Positive CSF RT-QuIC

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-010
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of severe, exhausting whole-body jerks and profound agitation in a bedbound patient.
Location: Private residence (Palliative setup)
Present at Visit: Patient and extended family caregivers
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage detailed violent, continuous myoclonic jerks triggered by basic care, alongside severe terminal agitation. Immediate assessment was required to exclude status epilepticus and administer urgent parenteral sedative/anti-epileptic therapy to alleviate extreme suffering and prevent caregiver injury before the in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "Every time we speak or turn on a light, he violently jerks and screams. He hasn't slept in two days and we can't manage him safely."
History of Present Illness: Patient has a known, recent diagnosis of rapidly progressive and universally fatal cognitive decline, having developed profound global dementia within a span of four months. Over the last 24 hours, there has been an acute exacerbation of prominent, stimulus-sensitive myoclonus, presenting as sudden, shock-like muscle jerks provoked by loud noises or sudden movement. The patient exhibits rapidly deteriorating psychiatric symptoms, currently manifesting as unpredictable extreme agitation. Early cerebellar signs and extrapyramidal features (rigidity) have progressed to near akinetic mutism, though currently disrupted by the severe myoclonus.
Functional Status: Bedbound; completely dependent for all care; non-communicative.
Social/Safety Concerns: Caregiver breakdown; physical injury to patient from violent myoclonic jerks against bedrails.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 160/90 mmHg | HR: 118 bpm | Temp: 37.2 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 60 Kg
General: Emaciated, profoundly agitated, exhibiting continuous, violent full-body myoclonic jerks.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination is limited by continuous movement. Severe, stimulus-sensitive myoclonus is instantly triggered by clapping hands or touching the limb (startle myoclonus). Severe spasticity and rigidity are evident in all four limbs between jerks. Pyramidal tract involvement is noted with a positive Babinski sign bilaterally. No signs of systemic infection. Documented history confirms positive CSF RT-QuIC and typical EEG changes for Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease.
Environmental Observations: Hospital bed in the living room; harsh overhead lighting exacerbating the startle response.
Medication Reconciliation: No specific anti-myoclonic agents currently prescribed. Subcutaneous line in situ.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease - Status: Terminal Exacerbation (Severe Myoclonus/Agitation)
Differentials:

Non-Convulsive Status Epilepticus - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Preservation of startle reflex nature of the jerks, no rhythmic clonic progression, and known terminal CJD trajectory.

Acute Delirium secondary to Sepsis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile, clear chest, and myoclonus being the dominant, specific neurological feature.

Medication Toxicity (e.g., Serotonin Syndrome) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of recent medication changes, clonus is startle-induced rather than spontaneous sustained clonus.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is in the terminal phase of a prion disease, currently suffering from catastrophic, uncontrolled stimulus-sensitive myoclonus and terminal agitation. The severity of the movement disorder is causing immense physical suffering to the patient and overwhelming distress to the family. Delaying assessment until the morning was ethically and clinically unacceptable, as the patient was at high risk of injury and complete exhaustion. Urgent after-hours assessment was definitively required to safely assess the level of consciousness, definitively rule out reversible causes of agitation, and urgently prescribe and administer high-dose parenteral benzodiazepines via the existing subcutaneous line to achieve immediate, compassionate symptom control.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Midazolam 5mg subcutaneously STAT. Prescribed a continuous subcutaneous infusion (Syringe Driver) via community palliative care containing Midazolam 30mg over 24 hours for myoclonus and agitation management.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Step 1: By lowering all lights, whispering, and moving very slowly, the family actively eliminates sensory triggers, instantly reducing the frequency of the violent myoclonic jerks.
Step 2: By padding the bed rails with soft blankets, the family proactively prevents bruising and skin tears during the involuntary spasms, ensuring the patient's physical integrity.
Step 3: By utilizing the prescribed subcutaneous breakthrough medication at the first sign of agitation, the family effectively suppresses the nervous system overactivity, guaranteeing the patient a peaceful, dignified rest.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent escalation to Specialist Palliative Care Service for morning review and syringe driver commencement.
DME/Safety (Optional): Bed rail protectors.
Follow-up: Palliative care team to take over daily management.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Do not call an ambulance for hospital transfer unless specifically directed by the palliative care team, as the goal of care is strict home-based comfort. Contact the 24-hour palliative care triage line for escalating symptoms.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The prognosis is exceptionally poor; the absolute priority of care is comfort and symptom suppression.
Clinical Course: The disease will continue to progress rapidly. The myoclonus and agitation will be heavily suppressed by the continuous medication infusion, likely leading to a peaceful, continuous sleep until death.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Contact the on-call palliative care nurse immediately if the patient appears to be in pain, if breathing becomes labored and distressing, or if the breakthrough medications fail to stop the jerking.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The brain damage from the disease is causing the nervous system to misfire violently at the slightest touch or sound. The Midazolam injection acts as a powerful brake, safely sedating the brain to stop the jerking and relieve all distress.
Actionable Steps: Keep the room completely dark and silent. Administer the subcutaneous breakthrough doses precisely as instructed. Await the palliative care team in the morning.

